1 NOTE {geni:about_me} =Hallkjell Jonson Huk=
2 CONT
2 CONT * Hallkjell Huk was a lover in Norway in the first half of the 12th century. Hallkjell belonged to Blindheimæten, a great-grandson from Sunnmøre, and was in service with the Norwegian king Harald Gille.
2 CONT
2 CONT ==[http://fmg.ac/Projects/MedLands/NORWEGIAN%20NOBILITY.htm#JonHalkelssonMMargareta Project MedLands NORWEGIAN NOBILITY]
2 CONT
2 CONT '''SIGRID Åsulvsdotter Rein married HALLKEL Huk and had two children:'''
2 CONT
2 CONT * 1      '''JON Halkelsson''' .  '''married MARGARETA Haraldsdatter, illegitimate daughter of HARALD "Gille" King of Norway & his mistress''' ---.  Snorre names "Harald Gille's third daughter…Margaret who was married to Jon Halkel
2 CONC son, a brother of Simon"[233]. 
2 CONT
2 CONT ''' Jon & his wife MARGARETA Haraldsdatter had one child:'''
2 CONT
2 CONT * (a)       '''HALLKEL Jonsson''' (- killed in battle Florevåg near Bergen 3 Apr 1194).  From Blindheim Jarl in Norway.  '''married (after 19 Jun 1179) as her second husband, RAGNHILD Erlingsdatter, widow of JON Thorbergsson, daug
2 CONC hter of ERLING Ormsen Skakke & his wife Kristin of Norway .'''  The Saga of King Sverre records the marriage of "Ragnhild daughter of Earl Erling and Kristin Kings-daughter" and "Halkel son of Jon Hallkelsson"[234].  
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Hallkel Jonsson & his wife Raghild  Erlingsdatter had one child:'''
2 CONT
2 CONT * (a)       '''RAGNVALD Hallkelsson''' (-murdered Husvik near Drøbak 1217, bur Oslo).  Stormann of the Bagler in Norway. 
2 CONT
2 CONT * 2 - '''SIMON Skalp''' (-killed in battle 1161).  Snorre names "Simon Skalp, a son of Halkel Huk" when recording his marriage[235].  '''married ([1155]) MARIA Haraldsdatter,''' illegitimate daughter of HARALD "Gille" King of Norw
2 CONC ay & his mistress ---.  Snorre names "Harald Gille's second daughter…Maria who was married to Simon Skalp, a son of Halkel Huk"[236].  
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Simon Skalp & his wife Maria Haraldsdatter had one child:'''
2 CONT
2 CONT * (a)       '''NIKOLAS'''  Snorre names Nikolas as son of "Simon Skalp, a son of Halkel Huk" & his wife[237].  Snorre records that "Simon Skalp's son, the son of a daughter of King Harald Gille" was proposed as king in 1161[238]. 
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT =History=
2 CONT
2 CONT Content Lendmann at Harald Gille 
2 CONT
2 CONT Hallkjell is known as the man who once obtained Harald Gille from Ireland in the 1120s. Harald had an Irish mother, claiming that his father was Magnus Berrføtt (Norwegian King 1093-1103).  Snorre tells in the story of Magnus Son 
2 CONC that Hallkjell traveled to the Sudrøyene ("the southern islands", the Norse name of the Hebrides), where he met Harald, who had then come from Ireland. Hallkjell brought both Harald and his mother to Norway and King Sigurd Jorsalf
2 CONC are. Sigurd was, if Harald's assertion of success was right, Harald's half brother. Sigurd demanded that Harald bear the burden of iron to prove his origin. Harald passed the test and was recognized as Sigurd's brother. 
2 CONT
2 CONT King Harald was killed in 1136, and the sons Sigurd, Inge and Øystein became kings. There were divisions in 1155 among the kingdoms, where Sigurd and Øystein merged with Inge to deprive him of the monarchy.  Both Hallkjell and his
2 CONC  sons first supported Øystein and Sigurd, but after Sigurd was killed later that year, both Hallkjell's sons went over to Inge.  King Øystein must have been very fond of money, and after a speech the king held after a few fights i
2 CONC n 1157, Snorre Hallkjell states the following: "Let your gold coffins now follow you and guard your country." Simon Skalp  After this we hear nothing more about Hallkjell, and maybe he died shortly after.
2 CONT
2 CONT Blindheimætten 
2 CONT
2 CONT Hallkjell belonged to the so-called Blindheim District, from Borgund in Sunnmøre. In the Middle Ages, the most powerful clans in the country made sure to marry each other, so the elite were linked together in a nationwide network.
2 CONC  The Blindheim District, and also the Giskeketen and the Rein family (the genus that lived on the farm Rein in Trøndelag), all originated from the Anglo-Saxon chief Skule Kongsfostre. Skule followed King Olav Kyrre from England to
2 CONC  Norway after the battle of Stamford bridge in 1066, and Hallkjell Huk was among Skule's many mighty descendants. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Hallkjell was the son of the lover Jon Smørbalte, and had two sons; Simon Skalp and Jon Hallkjellsson. Both sons were married to daughters of Harald Gille, Maria and Margaret, respectively. Jon's son, who also called Hallkjell, la
2 CONC ter became head of the Øyskjeggene, a group that fought against King Sverre Sigurdsson. 
2 CONT
2 CONT =Links=
2 CONT
